// chatgpt-image-ad owns the typography-heavy and UI-mimicry half of the static
// split. Its twin owns photoreal. Neither may ever answer for the other, which is
// why both descriptions carry an explicit refusal of the other's cues.
{"intent":"an ad that looks like an imessage thread between two founders","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad"}
{"intent":"make the creative look like a slack channel where someone recommends us","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad"}
{"intent":"a fake search results page with us sitting at the top","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad"}
{"intent":"mostly type: a big pull quote and a three column comparison against the competition","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad"}
{"intent":"render this one with gpt-image-2","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad"}
// Indirect: names no model and no style, but the whole creative is a UI.
{"intent":"an ad that looks like a screenshot of my notes app to do list","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad"}
// Both anchors in one sentence — a table is type, a bottle is a photo. Type wins:
// the glyphs are the thing that has to survive.
{"intent":"a comparison table ad with our bottle in the corner","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad","ambiguous_with":["nano-banana-image-ad"]}
// Dense copy with no model named. The catch-all takes an unanchored static; this
// one is anchored by how much text has to stay legible.
{"intent":"a Meta static with a lot of copy on it that still has to be readable","expected_skill":"chatgpt-image-ad","ambiguous_with":["novoads-api"]}
